    "chunk_text": "Why Sri Lanka\u2019s Major Reservoirs Cannot Prevent Floods  During A Severe Rainfall Event\n\nOur recent experience with the massive disaster brought by Cyclone Ditwah clearly indicated how vulnerable the country is to an extreme rainfall event. The impacts fell mainly into two categories: flooding across the major river basins and numerous landslides in the hill country. Many major river basins, including the Mahaweli, Kelani, Kalu, Maha, Attanagalu, and Deduduru, overflowed, affecting thousands of households and many cities and suburbs. Many criticisms were directed at the organisations responsible for reservoir management regarding how spill gates were operated during the event. While flood events are not new to the country, the question is whether we can cope with a similar or even more severe event in the future, especially given the finite reservoir storage available. This article discusses why Sri Lanka\u2019s major reservoirs cannot prevent floods during severe rainfall."
  },
