    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ebb6653cb1cd5ab058fe125c0870d5c0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1639,
    "chunk_text": "Extreme rainfall events produce catchment runoffs that far exceed reservoir storage itself. For instance, the recent Ditwah cyclone generated runoff flows in Deduru Oya, which is equivalent to several times of Deduru Oya reservoir storage. Hence, reservoirs can capture only a fraction of floodwater even if they are fully empty at the onset of the event. In other words, reducing the water levels of reservoirs before a severe flood event has no significant advantage.\n\nMany major river basins like Kelani, Attanagalu, Kalu, Gin, and Nilwala are not equipped with major reservoirs at all. The Kelani River has only minor reservoirs like Castlereagh and Maussakelle in the upstream, designed for hydropower rather than flood control. Therefore, heavy runoff in the Kelani always leads to flooding in Colombo and the suburbs, and only the flood bunds can control the flood risk to several areas. A similar situation can be observed in the Kalu, Gin, Nilwala, and Attanagalu basins as well.\n\nExtreme rainfall events can lead to dam overtopping unless spill gates are operated in time. A dam failure would cause a massive disaster due to the sudden release of a large volume of water at a higher velocity.\u00a0 As a result, a destructive flood wave will be generated, which is capable of sweeping away buildings, bridges, and other properties adjacent to the river, with heavy land erosion. Hence, it goes without saying that the damage would be manyfold compared with a flood event. Further, the disruption of the agricultural and power generation activities that depended upon the reservoir will add to the total economic loss in the long run."
  },
