    "chunk_text": "Kodakan Pillai v. Mudanayake, a landmark case and virtually the first constitutional challenge under the Soulbury Constitution, centred on the citizenship rights of persons of Indian origin in Sri Lanka.\u00a0Nadesan\u00a0represented Kodakan Pillai, whose name had been removed from the electoral register on the basis that he was not a citizen of Sri Lanka and lacked the qualifications required of an elector, even though he had been previously on the register and had voted at the elections conducted for the State Council. The removal was based on new legislation that made Sri Lankan citizenship a necessary qualification for an elector, effectively disenfranchising many Indian Tamils.\n\nThiagaraja v Karthigesu was a case of first impression in our courts that established judicial authority to issue the declaratory remedy regarding personal status. The court definitively affirmed its inherent jurisdiction to make a declaration of status, including marital status.\n\nIn the landmark\u00a0Press Council case before the Constitutional Court, Nadesan argued forcefully that the proposed\u00a0Press Council Bill\u00a0was fundamentally inconsistent with the First Republican Constitution of 1972. The significance of the issues raised was such that the Chairman of the Court,\u00a0T.S. Fernando, observed that it was the most important case he had heard during his tenure. Upholding Nadesan\u2019s argument that the 14-day period within which it was expected to deliver its decision was directory only, he said he would take even longer to allow the court sufficient time to deliberate fully and issue a considered opinion."
