    "chunk_size_bytes": 1755,
    "chunk_text": "The case of\u00a0Hewamanne v. de Silva is another landmark in Nadesan\u2019s career, and it raised intricate questions concerning the limits of\u00a0parliamentary privilege\u00a0and the application of the law of\u00a0contempt of court\u00a0to media reporting. A five-judge bench of the Supreme Court, chaired by Wanasundera J concluded that the contested publication constituted contempt, though they opted against imposing a penalty.\n\nIn a notable twist, Nadesan was himself charged with breaching parliamentary privilege.\n\nThis was not his first engagement with the law in this context. He had previously acted as counsel in\u00a0Attorney General v. Samarakkody, where he successfully defended a Member of Parliament facing a similar charge. His experience and foresight in drafting the legislation ultimately contributed to his own acquittal, validating his belief in the separation of powers.\n\nHe appeared before the Parliamentary Select Committee to defend another former Chief Justice Neville Samarakoon who was summoned to answer a charge of contempt of parliament arising from a speech he made at a prize giving ceremony at Sinnathurai Tutory in Colombo.\n\nOn 9 September 1983, Nadesan was addressing a five-judge Bench of the Supreme Court in a Saturday Review matter when the hearing was abruptly adjourned. It had come to light that the judges had taken their new oaths before one another rather than before the President, apparently contrary to the requirement of the Sixth Amendment. The judges did not turn up in court after the adjournment and the courts and judges\u2019 chambers were locked out, and armed guards barred entry. Confusion followed for several days. Fresh letters of appointment were subsequently issued, and each judge was required to take two new oaths."
