    "language": "en",
    "md5": "8e42ea6618bac427427aeb0f205dba14",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2001,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cDespite thousands of years of written and oral traditions involving crocodiles in Sri Lanka, not a single local source \u2014 not folklore, not hunting journals, not historical documents \u2014 mentions babies being used as bait. The only references come from sensationalist foreign newspapers, most likely self-propagated stories.\u201d\n\nThe research team examined centuries of material, from the Mah\u00e2vamsa to detailed accounts by British, Dutch and Portuguese naturalists and hunters.\n\nMost references to the alleged practice originated from a cluster of foreign newspapers, between 1888 and 1911. They often reprinted \u2014 without verification \u2014 the same anecdotes, illustrations or fictionalised accounts. Some even claimed local parents \u201crented out babies\u201d to English hunters.\n\nThe researchers found no record of the supposed advertisements in local newspapers, including the Ceylon Catholic Messenger, which foreign presses claimed had run a notice reading: \u201cBabies wanted for crocodile bait \u2014 will be returned alive.\u201d\n\nDr. de Silva explains: \u201cWe searched archives here and abroad. We accessed material through the Library of Congress and the National Library of Australia. No such advertisement exists. These appear to be inventions meant to entertain foreign readers with exoticised stories of the colonies.\u201d\n\nThe study also highlights how colonial narratives often portrayed colonised societies as \u201cprimitive,\u201d fueling racist stereotypes to justify imperial dominance.\n\nAccording to Dr. de Silva: \u201cThese stories fit into a broader pattern of colonial sensationalism, where exaggeration, or fabrication, was used to depict Asian and African societies as uncivilised. Unfortunately, some of these myths still survive, online, today.\u201d\n\nThe researchers point out that similar fabricated stories circulated about India and even the United States, where racist propaganda falsely depicted African-American children as \u201calligator bait\u201d \u2014 a slur weaponised during segregation."
