    "md5": "52aaea7e70e82e182e7d8491150b6ea4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1813,
    "chunk_text": "The researchers point out that similar fabricated stories circulated about India and even the United States, where racist propaganda falsely depicted African-American children as \u201calligator bait\u201d \u2014 a slur weaponised during segregation.\n\nDr. de Silva notes: \u201cThere are detailed accounts of hunters tying puppies or livestock near riverbanks to lure crocodiles. These are verified practices with multiple primary sources. But there is absolutely nothing comparable regarding human infants.\u201d\n\nThe authors argue that debunking colonial myths is essential not only for accuracy but also for shaping a respectful understanding of Sri Lanka\u2019s past.\n\nDr. de Silva adds: \u201cSuch sensationalist narratives distort our history and paint communities in an unfair light. Science must correct these misrepresentations.\u201d\n\nToday, Sri Lanka faces a very real human\u2013crocodile conflict, particularly in the Nilwala, Polonnaruwa and Hambantota regions. Dr. de Silva stresses that conservation solutions require sound science and community cooperation \u2014 not myths.\n\n\u201cBy understanding the real interactions between people and crocodiles \u2014 historically and today \u2014 we can better protect both human lives and an ancient species fighting for survival.\u201d\n\nThe exhaustive review by Dr. Anslem de Silva and Dr. Ruchira Somaweera firmly places the \u201cbaby-baiting\u201d stories in the realm of myth, misinformation and colonial sensationalism. Their work stands as a timely reminder of the importance of critical examination, especially when dealing with historical narratives crafted far from the lands they claim to describe.\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
